reg@orion.dwf.com () writes:
> 
> As far as I can tell, there is nothing like the sun '-r8' switch on either
> the fort (Dec/Compaq Fortran) or g77 compilers to default to 64 bit floats,
> and 128bit doubles.

Correct regarding g77, wrong regarding Compaq Fortran (fort). Compaq
Fortran has -r8 and -r16. g77 supports real*4 and real*8 (not
real*16), though.

> I SUSPECT that there is a way to get to 128bit (16byte) 'long double'
> arithmetic in both C and Fortran, but the simple guess of 'long double'
> with gcc still produced an 8 bit size.  
> Any thoughts here?  

It seems long double is nyi (defaults to double) with C, but Fortran
implements real*16 (and the -r16 switch). A simple check also verifies
that

	real*16 x(2)
	write(*,*) loc(x(2)) - loc(x(1))

returns 16.

> 
