Go to compaq's website: www.service.digital.com   - in downloadable drivers & utilities select alpha systems.  This brings up the firmware
update page.  Near the bottom you can find SRM console for PC164 (I have a 366 MHz AlphaPC164 system from Enorex).  Select PC164 gives you
the page with release notes, installation instructions and the fwupdate.exe program and pc164srm.rom. Both the fwupdate.exe and pc164srm.rom
fit on a dos format floppy , which you put in your floppy drive and then in the ARC (PC164 does NOT have AlphaBIOS, it has ARC) menus you
can find firmware update.  It will reset the console and then load the floppy.  It will flash the ROM and then tell you the console
selection (Windows NT) does not match loaded console (OpenVMS) and do you want to change it, you say yes (or select OpenVMS, it's been a
little while) and then you are done and it will have you power cycle the machine and you'll get the nice AlphPC164 SRM and >>>  prompt.
Caveat:  my machine was IDE only and SRM does not show the IDE controller but it does show the 2 IDE disks I have on my primary
IDE channel.  It does NOT show the IDE cdrom on the secondary channel.  I have added SCSI card and SCSI CD-ROM & disk (for booting
Tru64/OpenVMS/*BSD) and I've booted RedHat 6.1 from my SCSI CD-ROM and installed to one of the IDE disks.  I had to wipe the disk clean from
an earlier RH 5.2 in order that the redhat installer generated a new BSD disklabel for SRM booting, otherwise it would use the DOS style
partition table which is not SRM bootable.  I can boot the system from SRM second IDE disk on primary ide channel:  boot dqa1 -fi 2/vmlinuz
-flags "root=/dev/hdb1 ro"
I can set the boot environment variables so it will boot automatically (I believe).  set boot_def  dqa1
set boot_file 2/vmlinuz  set boot_osflags "root=/dev/hdb1 ro".

Dirk




