Newton Love <newtlove@mindspring.com> wrote in message
news:895620$h2f$1@nntp6.atl.mindspring.net...
> As I said elswewhere:
> Quick method: Code s use an integer to keep track of the offset in
> a file. A 32 bit system can't address more than 2^32 bytes offset.
> In a 64 bit system, it can address 2^64. So, find yourself a great
> big drive, and use cat to make a really huge file. Then use a code to
> open it, lseek to the end, and tell you what is the final offset.
>
> I just finished a sort on a 23 GByte file. Took forever, but it worked.

You might also try this one: Create a large file (>4Gb). Memorymap
the entire file. Then access byte number somewhere beyond 4Gb.
This is physically impossible if you have only 32 bits of address
space. A 32-bit system could use 64-bit file pointers (although
i386 Linux lseek indeed returns a 32-bit value), but cannot map
a >4Gb file as a whole.
I tried it on a 5 Gb file. It worked.


