>However, in looking at the
>documentation on how to configure X it seems to imply that if you installed
>"server" or did a custom install without the X components you can't run the
>"Configurator".

As far as I know the X-windows system, it consists of two parts: the
X-server and the window manager. The X-server is a program like
XF86_SVGA, XF86_TGA, and the window manager is a program like fvwm, twm,
olwm.

You may check whether an X-server is installed by 

rpm -qa | sort | more

Then you should find XFree86_SVGA, or XF86_SVGA, or another server. If
not, then you do not have an X-server installed. That would be weird, as
I cannot imagine a server installation without the proper X-windows
parts.

If you look at
http://www.uni-paderborn.de/mirrors/xfree86/cardlist.html, then you will
find a list of which server to use for which card. The problem is that
this is a list for intel machines.

From
http://www.uni-paderborn.de/mirrors/xfree86/3.3.6/RELNOTES10.html#14
comes the following information:

>The following X servers are available for Alpha hardware: 
>
>     XMa64.tgz       Mach 64 server
>     XMono.tgz       Mono server (generic driver only)
>     XP9K.tgz        P9000 server
>     XTGA.tgz        DEC 21030 (TGA) server
>     XS3.tgz         S3 server
>     XS3V.tgz        old S3 ViRGE server (please use SVGA server)
>     XSVGA.tgz       SVGA server (Matrox Millennium and S3 ViRGE drivers only)

So, it is questionable whether other video cards (like my Trident) will
work for X-windows using XFree86.

Use XF86_SVGA --help to view which switch tells you for which cards
XF86_SVGA is compiled; I believe it is --showconfig.

Maybe compiling the server including the right driver will work, maybe
it will fry your alpha, I do not know. There must be a reason why those
drivers are not compiled into the server. The guys at XFree must be able
to tell more.

Marten Jan



