bmeyer@cs.monash.edu.au wrote:
: Ron Farrer <rfarrer@my-deja.com> writes:
: 
: >No, Alpha is a 64-bit microprocessor. It can run 64-bit and 32-bit
: >compiled code. Alpha supports both little and big endian (in hopes
: >people will stop asking that one...)
: 
: Actually, unless the 21264 introduced something new, the "support"
: for the non-native endianness is pretty much minimal. There isn't even
: an instruction to shuffle the bytes around the way you'd need.

On some microprocessors (I'm not sure if Alpha does this way), there is a pin
on the processor chip that selects little vs. big endian mode.  So, by
grounding the pin you get one endian, and by pulling it up to Vcc, you get the
other endian.

        Stu



