re: true parity memory for AS200

Check pricewatch (www.pricewatch.com).  I've paid $195 for a pair of 
64MB SIMMS, and $90 for a pair of 32MB from different vendors I found
through that site.  Just be sure when you call the vendor that you
are getting 70ns (or 60ns) _TRUE_PARITY_ (not logic parity).  The
sticks with 12-18 chips tend to cost more than the ones with 36 chips
but in my experience its worth the difference in life expectancy and
the lower profile leaving room for cabling.

Note that many older 486 systems, generally "server class" used true
parity memory; I have a pair of Compaq 64MB SIMMS in a multia now.
Be careful about using mismatched contact types (the multia wants and
got gold contact simms, the AS200 wants tin simms).  That can be a
source for cheaper memory.  I've seen folks selling 486-based servers
complete for less than the memory in them is worth.

Rich Jordan

P.S., while officially unsupported, I know of folks who have installed
three pairs of 128MB SIMMS in their AS200 systems for a total of 768MB.
OpenVMS recognized it and used it, don't know about other OS's.




