In article <Bryan.Walls-DCB872.21305115022000@news>, Bryan Walls <Bryan.Walls@msfc.nasa.gov> writes:
>
>The system has two identical graphics cards installed in PCI slots that 
>worked under NT. SuperProbe claims to find one, and that it is a Cirrus 
>CL-GD5424 with 512kB of memory. The board is labeled as a Digital part. 
>The part number is 50-23480-01. I couldn't find anything on the card 
>that matched with listings of DEC cards.

It sounds as if you may have the TGA2 (PBXGB or 3D30) card, which
is not supported under Linux (at least for any release I've seen
so far).  Do the cards have a rotary switch on the side?  Or at
the SRM prompt do a SHOW CONFIG and see what it says for video.
If you have the switch or if SRM says TGA2, it won't work (yet?)
for Linux.

I've found that SuperProbe thinks they're some kind of Cirrus
card, but that definition doesn't work.

I suspect the easiest way out is to get another card.  Most S3
cards work now with RH 6.1.  In particular, cards that may be
found in AlphaStations that I know work are the PCXAG, and the
S3 Trio64/Trio32 (NumberNine Motion 331).  The documentation lists
a long line of other cards that will work.  Even an old ISA board
will do fine if you're not going to pound of the graphics very
heavily.

