Trust us.  It's 64-bit...

What is it that you want to do with the box?  How would you
'test' the 64-bit-edness in any case?  There are several types
that are now 64-bit, like 'long', 'size_t' and 'time_t'.
Double precision math is almost the same speed as single-
precision, although with lots of other processors, this is
the case as well.  The kernel version of strlen does 8 bytes
at a time, various other things are 64-bit.  There are 64-bit
values in /proc/*/stat.  'primes' keeps running past 2^32
and goes to about 2^62 (Kids: don't try this at home =-).  

If you installed Linux on an alpha, it's a 64-bit system.
I think it still takes a special kernel option to support
more than 2GB of memory, but you can't put that much on an
EB164 anyway.

It's kind of an odd question, like 'prove that the sky is
blue when it's sunny out'

Anyway, hope this helps,

Tony Hammitt


