Craig Newstrom (cjn@usjet.net) wrote:
: I'd thought that the fact that the alpha is a 64bit machine alone
: would be proof.  Isn't it true that ANY OS you run on this
: will be running 64bit?

No.  This depends on what address range is actually used by OS.

: DecUnix, Linux, VMS, NT, whatever, if you run it on a 64bit
: machine - is it running 64bit mode, because its a 64bit
: machine?

NT on Alpha runs in 32-bit mode.  It was promissed a number of
times that "the next version" will be 64-bit (maybe).  As support for
NT on Alpha was dropped it will remain that way.

DU (a.k.a. OSF/1, now known as Tru64) compilers have switches (-taso)
which allow to compile applications with addressing cut down to 32 bits.
This is normally used to circumvent buggy assumptions in sources about
relative sizes of various types.  Netscape for DU is compiled that
way, for example.  In such case even if OS remains in 64 bit mode
an application is using only a subset of available resources.

There are similar, quite unofficial :-), modificiations available
for Linux tools (ftp://ftp.park.msu.ru/ink/).  You would probably need
another set of libraries, compiled in the same way, to push that concept
to its limits.

: Or can it run differently?

Yeap.

   Michal  



