In article <38AFED93.A318597A@tstc.edu>,
  rdooley@tstc.edu wrote:
> Correct me if I'm wrong, but NT isn't truly 64 bit on alpha. W2k on
> itanium will be though.

Correct, NT on any current (x86) or past (Alpha, SPARC, MIPS)
architecture is 32-bit. In fact I would not be at all surprised if it
still contained bits of 16-bit code somewhere...

Someone else wrote:
> > I'd thought that the fact that the alpha is a 64bit machine alone
> > would be proof.  Isn't it true that ANY OS you run on this
> > will be running 64bit?
> > DecUnix, Linux, VMS, NT, whatever, if you run it on a 64bit
> > machine - is it running 64bit mode, because its a 64bit
> > machine?
> >
> > Or can it run differently?

No, Alpha is a 64-bit microprocessor. It can run 64-bit and 32-bit
compiled code. Alpha supports both little and big endian (in hopes
people will stop asking that one...)

Alpha supports many different types of data formats (taken from Alpha
Architecture Handbook (any typos are mine)):

"1.2 Data Format Overview

Alpha is a load/store RISC architecture with the following data
characteristics:

   All operations are done between 64-bit registers.
   Memory is accessed via 64-bit virtual byte addresses, using the
little-endian or, optionally, the bit-endian byte numbering convention.
   There are 32 integer registers and 32 floating-point registers.
   Longword (32-bit) and quadword (64-bit) integers are supported.
   Five floating-point data types are supported:
   - VAX F-floating (32-bit)
   - VAX G-floating (64-bit)
   - IEEE single (32-bit)
   - IEEE double (64-bit)
   - IEEE extended (128-bit)"

Alpha also supports 8, 16, 32, and 64-bit integers.

Tru64 UNIX, Linux, FreeBSD, NetBSD, and OpenBSD are all 64-bit operating
systems on Alpha. Tru64 UNIX and Debian GNU/Linux 2.2 (potato/frozen)
support a special linker flag (-taso) that forces code into the lower
32-bit memory address space. This is used for code that otherwise breaks
on Alpha, such as Netscape Communicator.


JMO,

