Ron Sokoloski (rsokoloski1@home.com) wrote:
: You have to move the symbolic links "vmlinuz" and "System.map: to point to
: the new files:

That would be the other way around than in commands quoted below.

: # cd /boot
: # rm vmlinuz
: # rm System.map
: # ln -s vmlinuz  vmlinuz-2.2.14.gz
: # ln -s System.map System.map-2.2.14

: The kernel link isn't as important as the system map link.

As a matter of fact this system map link is not only unimportant
but actually harmfull if you are booting multiple kernels.  If you
will store your system map in /boot/System.map-<ext>, where <ext>
is the same as name of a directory created by 'make modules_install',
then symbols will be found in separate files by each of kernels
you are booting.  Just make sure that EXTRAVERSION is different in
a top kernel Makefile if you have multiple instances (different
configuration, or patches) of the same version of a kernel.

Also you may call you kernel absolutely any way you please. 'vmlinuz'
is not magical in any sense.

  Michal




