G. Hugh Song wrote:
> 
> Compaq ported Mesa into the rpm format on Alpha/Linux for
> everyone to grab.
> 
> I am looking for Fortran-callable graphics routines for
> scientific contour/3-D surface plotting.  Does Mesa have
> appropriate routines in the library?  It appears Mesa can do
> far more complicated things than just 3-D surface plotting.
> 

   Mesa is an OpenGl implementation so you use OpenGl
  instructions (and also Glut's ones to deal with windows
  events : OpenGl do only rendering). If it is relatively 
  easy to plot some coloured triangles, contour-lines (up to
  my knowledge of OpenGl) must be coded. Perhaps you may
  find somebody who have already coded some routines which
  do the stuff. Nevertheless OpenGl is really a powerfull
  way to do every thing about graphics but with some efforts.
     
   Another problem is that OpenGl is for rendering on
  a screen. If you can easily grab a window (for instance with
  xv) and transfert it in a graphical format (postscript, gif, ...)
  the obtained picture have the screen resolution which may be
  to low for quality publishing. About this subject you may
  found (in the C examples which come with mesa) a routine
  which is a postscript driver that used the graphical representation
  of an OpenGl object before the last operations (as one of theses
  is the hidden faces elimination with the depth buffer algorithm,
  the triangular faces have to be ordered so as to obtain this effect
  in postscript)

   f90 examples codes come with the f90 OpenGl bindings :

   http://math.nist.gov/f90gl/

   A last point : mesa generaly transforms OpenGl instructions in
  X11 instructions (*) and X11 is basically 2d : this means that in this
  case, mesa don't use 3d capability of the hardware and rendering may
be
  slow (nevertheless it is usable : I am happy to have mesa for my linux
  box !). 

    
  (*) they are some drivers for some graphical chips (Voodoo, and
others)
  and in this case mesa may used 3d capabilities of the graphical board.

