Note that there is an Alpha port of Debian, which is being quite
actively worked on.

The most major problem with this port is that the Alpha platform has a
fair variety of hardware, some of which needs *rather* customized
startup code (e.g. - some combination of ARC, SRM, MILO).  The nasty
part of a Linux/Alpha install is, quite frankly, the part that takes
place before you have Linux running.  There are several variations on
the hardware, and they have substantially different bootup procedures.

It doesn't seem likely to me that Corel would find it financially
worthwhile to put a lot of effort into building "user friendlier"
installation tools for this.  This would involve fairly substantial
effort (and probably represents a *remarkably* large portion of what
Corel has coded for the IA-32 "Corel Linux" port), and significant
effort would be required to set up "user friendlier" *INSTRUCTIONS.*

Beyond that, much of Corel Linux is composed of a tiny subset of
Debian packages; Debian/Alpha has considerably more packages than does
Corel Linux.

I suggest that you may find it more interesting to pursue the
Debian/Alpha distribution.
