Centuries ago, Nostradamus foresaw a time when Nathan McEachen would say:
>I spoke with some representatives from Oracle at Oracle OpenWorld 1998.  At
>the time, the guys from Oracle said that there were no plans to port Oracle
>8 over to Alpha Linux.  I really don't know if their position has changed
>since then, but if anyone knows then please let me know.  I think it would
>be to their advantage to have their product ported over to the fastest
>Linux platform.

The problem with that is that Oracle performance is *highly* tied to
I/O bandwidth rather than to CPU speed.

The place where Alpha is *really* strong is in handling of FP; that
tends to have little to do with Oracle as a DBMS.

The place where there *COULD* be major benefit to "going Alpha" would
be in its ability to manage very large memory spaces.  It's not clear,
though, that Alpha systems can have substantially larger amounts of
memory than a Xeon-based system.  64 bits is nice in theory, but if:
  a) You can only get at 32 bits worth of PCI bus, and
  b) Supported hardware maxes out at ~2GB of RAM,
this is not better than what can be done on IA-32.

None of these are insurmountable problems, and there may even be
partial answers now.  But that doesn't add up to Oracle expecting to
be able to make a few million dollars licensing an Linux/Alpha port of
Oracle 8i.
-- 
