To build my custom kernels I install the kernel sources in my own dir tree..
example
/usr/local/src/linux-2.2.14
then
cd /usr/local/src
then
ln -s ./linux-2.2.14/linux ./linux

That creates /usr/local/src/linux   which is a link to
/usr/local/src/linux-2.2.14/linux
To build a kernel I  "cd  /usr/local/src/linux" then issuue the build
comands (make config, make dep clean boot)

Then each time I build against a new version kernel source I just recreate
the /usr/local/src/linux link to the latest  release I installed, my old
builds are still there, and the redhat sources in usr/src/ are untouched.

Just an fyi..

JJ
