Organization: Lockheed Martin Corporation
Mime-Version: 1.0
Reply-To: csernica@ihwy.com
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

Travis Wheatley wrote:

> OSLOADFILENAME=\images\generic.img

This is where you're going wrong.  This is a disk image, such as you
might use for creating a boot floppy, and not a MILO image.  Milo.img is
also a disk image.  In fact, looking over the documentation available on
Red Hat's site, you can't boot from the CD when installing on an Alpha. 
You need to create kernel, MILO, and RAMdisk diskettes.  This is what
the images are for.

I've never done this myself (I run Debian, not Red Hat) so I can't
really tell you what to do from my own experience.  (With Debian, you
*can* boot from the CD on most machines.)  Go to
http://www.redhat.com/support/manuals/ and get the Installation Guide
and the Alpha Installation Addendum for details on how to proceed.  The
Alpha Installation Addendum looks to be only available as a gzipped
Postscript file or tgzipped HTML archive.

You do appear to be one of the lucky ones, though.  With the EB boards,
you can blow MILO into flash memory and boot from there.  On most Alphas
you can't boot from a version of MILO written onto a Linux partition
because the BIOS doesn't understand how to read Linux partitions by
itself.  You have to create a small FAT partition or boot MILO from a
FAT-formatted diskette instead - but you won't have to do that if you
can figure out how to blow MILO into flash.

-- Chris Csernica



