|> > system libraries?
|> 
|> It can. If you're trying to access a 16-bit datum via a pointer that points
|> to an odd byte address on e.g. a SPARC, you'll get an alignment interrupt
|> which can, depending on the compiler switches, result in a segmentation
|> fault. Similarly, accessing a 32-bit entity on a non-four-bytye-aligned
|> address causes the same thing.

Unaligned accesses usually cause bus errors (SIGBUS), not segfaults.

