"Brad Stewart" <bradstewart1@home.com> writes:

> Greetings all,
> 
> Well, I finally got my Multia to boot up and start X.  It took awhile, but
> with some help things have panned out.  The docs that I have been using I
> got from RedHat's web page.  I have installed RH6.1 running with 64 meg of
> RAM and a 2 gig SCSi hard drive.  During the install process, I setup swap
> to be 64M, my root partition (/) to be 1979 (on /dev/sda1), and I reserved
> 1M for a 16 bit dos partition (on /dev/sda6).  I did this so I could dd my
> floppy there and boot off the hard drive (just like the docs say).  However,
> when I go to set up the ARC console to point to partition 6 where my MILO
> and linload.exe live, I get the following error:  Bad device name/number or
> partition number.  I'm not sure how to get around this error.  If anyone
> knows what I may be doing wrong, I would appreciate it.
> 
You are doing nothing wrong - it's the disk partitioner.  On a Multia, the
fdisk follows PC-style rules, which allow for four primary partitions and
some number of secondary partitions.  sda1 is the first primary partition,
sda2-sda4 are never used (don't exist), sda5-sdaX are secondary partitions.
From Linux, no problems.  From MILO, you must count only those partitions
that were actually created.  So if you put MILO on /dev/sda6, then from the
ARC console you need to point to disk partition 3 (1=sda1, 2=sda5, 3=sda6)
when configuring.

> Also, on a side note, I am running the KDE desktop and when I move the
> curser over the gnome icon on the KDE menu bar, my box locks up.  I can
> still move the mouse, but everything else is locked down.  Everything else
