Eberhard Burr (Eberhard.Burr@gmx.de) wrote:

: my first audio disk with cdrdao. Trying to use xcdroast, I found that
: cdwrite won't work because it asks the kernel for 4MB of shared memory
: and the max size is defined to be slightly less.

Actually it is defined as 4MB but there is a small overhead.  OTOH
cdrecord (not cdwrite) works just dandy with a fifo size set to slightly
smaller 4000k, instead of a default 4M, and there are described in
documentation flags and environment variables which allow you to do just
that.

: After changing the
: #define in /include/asm-alpha/shmparam.h and recompiling the kernel,
: it appears to work nicely, though.

You work too hard.  It works equally nicely without recompiling your
kernel.  Besides with 2.2 kernels you can change this amount of shared
memory by writing, with 'echo', a required value to
/proc/sys/kernel/shmmax.

   Michal



