Alexander Meisel (meisel@ai-lab.fh-furtwangen.de) wrote:
: 'rpm -e' works, but youre right there is no dep. checking.

Yes, indeed.  I was too fast too type.  It "works" due to, once again,
of "unistall" shell scripts.  That means that if you have a conflict with
a file from some other package then it will be quietly gone after
'rpm -e' without traces of a deed in your rpm database.  This is
indeed a theoretical consideration in this moment but if such style of
packaging would spread that would undermine the whole reason to have rpm
in the first place.

In other words this package is really something like 'shar' pretending
that it is an rpm archive which, on the top of everything, leaves
behind totally unneeded after installation over 16 MB in files
/usr/local/netscape/netscape-all-4.7.spec
/usr/local/netscape/netscape-all-4.7.tar.gz
which are **the only** ones actually registered in rpm database. Ouch!

   Michal



