"Dr. Unk" <dr_unk@hotmail.com> wrote in message
news:876gcd$96v$1@nnrp1.deja.com...
> 4.  Does it have anything to do with architecture dependent code or
> system libraries?

It can. If you're trying to access a 16-bit datum via a pointer that points
to an odd byte address on e.g. a SPARC, you'll get an alignment interrupt
which can, depending on the compiler switches, result in a segmentation
fault. Similarly, accessing a 32-bit entity on a non-four-bytye-aligned
address causes the same thing.

For 64-bit processors, this is much more likely. I notice you've posted this
to the alpha newsgroup, so this may be relevant. I've just finished porting
some code to Sparc Ultra and our code was sprinkled with just such
misaligned code, despite it being OK for 32-bit alignment. The problem is
that a long is now 64 bits, which screwed everything up.

On machines with Intel processors, this doesn't happen.

B=





